WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.320
SpaceX's FCC filing for an orbital data center constellation brings reentry debris and collision

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.460
risks under formal regulatory review for the first time. This is SpaceX Daily, episode

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:19.120
106. SpaceX filed an analysis with the Federal Communications Commission covering reentry

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:25.980
debris and collision risks for the proposed star-mind orbital data center constellation. The submission

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:31.140
Adresse's end-of-life disposal requirements and on-orbit conjunction probabilities for

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.980
the plan's satellite network. Reports indicate the filing marks the initial regulatory step toward

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:42.840
licensing an on-orbit compute platform. The document provides detailed assessments of how the

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:48.760
satellites would be de-orbited at the end of their operational life. It also evaluates the likelihood

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.280
of collisions with other objects in low-earth orbit during the constellation's lifetime.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:01.040
time. According to satnews.com, that filing is now public. Bank of America estimated $160

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:08.140
billion to deliver 1 gigawatt of power generation capacity in orbit. Space X's internal planning

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:14.720
for the same capability targets under $1 billion. The gap stems from differences in assumed launch

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:21.520
cadence, hardware reuse rates, and propellant costs. Analysts at the bank assume single-use vehicles

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.000
and limited flight rates when building their model.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.120
SpaceX planning instead factors in repeated booster flights

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.300
and industrial scale propellant pricing.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.160
Next big future.com laid out the contrast between the two approaches.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.360
Clean view analysis projects US data center power capacity

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.380
will rise by a factor of 7 over the coming decade.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.100
The forecast ties directly to training and inference demand

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.540
for large language models.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:53.180
Regional grid interconnection cues already show multi-year backlogs in several states.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:59.020
The report highlights that current permitting processes cannot keep pace with projected load growth.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.080
Several states now face interconnection delays exceeding three years for new facilities.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.660
Cryptobreafing.com covered the clean view numbers.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:15.040
Timelapse imagery documents rapid construction of multiple hyperscale facilities in Virginia, Texas, and Arizona.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.360
each site adds hundreds of megawatts of new load within 18 months of groundbreaking.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.440
Satellite and aerial photography confirmed the pace exceeds prior five-year

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.620
build cycles. Photographers captured substation expansions that

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:34.040
doubled local transformer capacity in under 12 months at several locations.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.440
The images also show evaporative cooling towers installed at rates

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.960
not seen in previous industrial projects.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.060
HetaPixel.com published the before and after photography. Redwire supplies components

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:52.800
for both Starship and Legacy launch vehicles. Analysts note the company's valuation hinges

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.240
on Starship flight rate assumptions versus slower growth in established programs. The

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.120
stock trades at a premium appears that do not carry equivalent Starship exposure. Investors

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:10.660
must weigh the upside from rapid Starship cadence against execution risk on the new vehicle. Redwire's

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:15.400
existing contracts with legacy programs provide a baseline revenue stream independent

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.740
of starship success.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.920
The Motley Fool examined the red wire exposure.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:25.500
The analysis compares proposed AI training floor space per worker against historical

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.900
ICE detention density standards.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.520
At current rack densities, the same square footage could theoretically house far more occupants

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.400
under deportation planning metrics.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:40.320
The comparison highlights permitting and labor policy intersections with data center

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:40.820
sighting.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:46.380
The study uses publicly available rack power and floor area figures to derive the density

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:46.920
ratios.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.160
It notes that cooling and power infrastructure requirements differ sharply between the two

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:52.780
use cases.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.320
FlyingPenguin.com ran the density comparison.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:02.120
The family of a worker who died on the first day at the Memphis Colossus 2 site filed a

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.540
$30 million lawsuit.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.600
The complaint alleges inadequate fall protection during initial construction activities.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.580
The incident occurred at the XAI facility expansion adjacent to existing colossus clusters.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.400
Court documents list the worker's employer as a subcontractor on the project.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.480
The suit seeks damages for alleged negligence in safety protocol enforcement.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.680
Hoodline.com reported the lawsuit filing.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.040
A Falcon 9 mission from Vandenberg's Space Force Base remains on the schedule for tonight.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.640
The launch window opens after local sunset and closes before midnight.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:43.440
Observers along the central California coast may see the booster return burn if weather permits.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.580
The mission carries a batch of Starlink V2 mini satellites.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.280
Recovery of the first stage is planned on a drone ship stationed in the Pacific.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.720
Ventura County Star has the launch tracker details.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:06.420
Observers on X noted the FVC filing for Starmind as the first public document linking Starlink orbital slots to compute payloads rather than communications.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:12.640
One post highlighted the collision risk section as unusually detailed for an initial application.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:21.780
Another user flagged the reentry analysis as evidence that SpaceX is treating the constellation under the same disposal rules as Starlink shells.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:27.240
The discussion thread collected over 200 replies within the first hour of posting.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:33.320
A thread on Reddit compared the Bank of America gigawatt cost figure to the actual propellant

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.280
and steel mass required for equivalent Starship flights.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.800
Commenters calculated that even at current Raptor production rates, the hardware cost

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.060
alone stays well below the bank estimate.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:50.320
Several users pointed out that the $1 billion target assumes full booster reuse and rapid

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:55.340
pad turnaround. The thread reached the front page of the sub-reddit within four hours.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.920
Photos of new data center construction in northern Virginia drew comments on the visible scale

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:06.760
of substation upgrades. One observer counted eight new transformer banks installed in a single

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:12.980
month at one site. Another noted that local water permits for evaporative cooling now exceed prior

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:18.940
industrial allocations in the same county. The images prompted debate over whether grid capacity

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.520
can support the projected 7-fold increase.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:27.640
One thing worth watching is the $30 million lawsuit filed after the fatal fall at the Memphis

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.580
Colossus 2 construction site.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:34.640
The complaint claims insufficient safety measures were in place on the workers first day.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:40.680
Resolution will depend on whether the investigation finds violations of OSHA fall protection standards

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.780
or gaps in contractor oversight.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:48.340
The outcome could affect permitting timelines for the remaining phases of the XAI campus.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:54.400
On the AI front, SpaceX submitted the first regulatory package for the Star-Mind Orbital

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.900
Data Center constellation, directly tying launch capacity to on-orbit compute hardware.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:05.280
The filing covers both debris mitigation and conjunction assessment for the planned satellite

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:05.780
network.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:12.120
CleanView's seven-fold power capacity forecast for U.S. data centers underscores the terrestrial

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:15.080
constraints that make orbital placement attractive.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:20.980
The Memphis Colossus 2 lawsuit highlights construction risks at the scale required to support

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:22.820
next-generation training clusters.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:28.460
Engineering deep dive on the orbital compute cost model starts with the reuse multiplier.

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:34.860
If a super heavy booster flies 20 times instead of once, the amortized cost of the tankage,

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.940
engines, and avionics drops by that same factor.

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:44.180
Erpellent is the next lever. Liquid oxygen and methane are bulk commodities whose raw material

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:50.560
price sits near 20 cents per kilogram at industrial scale. A single starship stack carries roughly

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:58.280
1,200 tons of propellant. At 20 reuses, that mass cost is spread across 20 missions. The remaining

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:04.440
variable is pad and tower throughput. Each additional flight per month multiplies the effective power

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:10.460
delivery rate without adding new steel or silicon. When those three numbers, reuse count,

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:16.460
propellant price, and flight cadence move together, the delivered cost per watt in orbit falls

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:21.980
by two orders of magnitude from the single use ground up build assumed in the higher estimate.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:28.000
The same physics that collapsed launch prices now applies to placing and servicing compute hardware

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.800
where sunlight is constant and radiators face deep space.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:37.700
Heat rejection in orbit requires only large, lightweight panels, rather than the massive

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.980
chillers and water loops demanded on Earth.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:46.840
Power generation can rely on simple solar arrays whose mass scales linearly with output, eliminating

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.780
the need for fuel logistics after an initial deployment.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.660
The regulatory filing submitted this week shows SpaceX already modeling the end-of-life

00:08:55.660 --> 00:09:00.740
disposal mass that must be de-orbited, which directly informs how many additional flights

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:05.680
will be needed for constellation maintenance. Those extra flights become part of the same

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:11.620
reuse economics that drive the sub-1 billion dollar target. The engineering therefore reduces

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:16.840
to a closed loop. Launch cost, reuse rate, and on-orbit servicing cadence,

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:22.000
determine whether orbital compute can undercut terrestrial power economics by the factor

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:32.500
the numbers suggest. A quick look at the tape shows SPCX closed at $152.71, down 1.6%.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:37.460
Before we go, keep an eye on the Vandenberg launch window tonight for any last minute holds

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:42.720
that could shift the Starlink deployment cadence. That's the day at SpaceX. That's a wrap.

